well on this Veterans Day we look back  at those military members who seek a  life in the civilian world after serving  some struggling to adapt like the case  of Andrew ho tonight he shares his  journey and what helped him get through  this process  I wanted to serve just because I wanted  to make my parents proud thirty-year-old  Andrew ho enlisted in the Air Force and  served for seven years when his contract  ended he says continuing his education  was his goal but adjusting to civilian  life was not easy it was a little rough  I didn't really know too many people  obviously all my high school friends  already graduated college so it's a  different experience that's where  executive director Ross Bryant comes in  spearheading unlv's Military and  Veterans Services Center he says its  focus is to help every generation of  veterans come back to the classroom if  you think of like the Vietnam generation  there was not a lot of services to help  in their transition and every generation  has a transition whether you did 25  years like me those Services include  admission VA education benefits  financial aid and employment  opportunities UNLV also recognized  nationally for being military friendly  as a top 10 school for its diverse  programs these vets are coming back  after some of them fighting a war and  being in a culture where you are on a  Super Bowl team and you have that  camaraderie where everyone takes care of  you when you have a challenge and you  help you take care of others today ho  serves as a mentor for incoming student  vets and has been nationally recognized  for his leadership as the country honors  those that served on Veterans Day ho  says it's the love from the community  which makes it all worth it just to give  back what means is giving back being  appreciated  will the military and Veterans Services  Center just celebrated their 10th  anniversary and their campus office is  full of unlimited resources the same  tools that host has is helping